// joints — import topology, drafted by `zoning draft` from the real
// @import graph. Every line below is TRUE of the tree as it stands: this is
// a description, not a wish. Read it once, then start tightening it —
// merging two zones or sealing a directory is where the value is.

package joints {
    root     src
    language zig
    // Three faces, not one. `root.zig` is the module's public door and imports
    // everything because that is what a facade does. `proof.zig` is the test
    // build's root and imports everything for the same structural reason in the
    // other direction: a test is reachable to `zig build test` only if something
    // names it, and naming all of them is its entire job. `idiom.zig` is the third
    // root — `zig build idiom` — and reaches every module for a third reason: a
    // claim about the whole package's vocabulary has nowhere narrower to stand.
    // Listing them here is what lets the seals below mean something — a seal every
    // file may cross is not a seal, and the alternative was writing
    // `open to proof.zig` on each of them, which says the same thing twice and
    // once per seal added later.
    facade   root.zig proof.zig idiom.zig
}

// Zones, low to high: an import may not point up the page. The order is a
// topological sort of what imports what today, so nothing points up yet.
zones {
    // The generator, and the floor of the package: `press` reads nothing above
    // it. Every zone from here up to `folio` reads it for the grammar, the LALR
    // tables, and the LR(0) core. Its four layers are one zone because they are
    // one area — the partition inside `press/` is a DAG the directories enforce
    // on their own (`copy` reads nothing, `cast` reads `copy`, `quarrel` reads
    // both, and the root reads all three), so naming them separately here would
    // buy an ordering the tree already keeps. `docket/` is the exception and is
    // spelled out below, which is why this line enumerates rather than globbing.
    press        press/*.zig press/copy/** press/cast/** press/quarrel/**
    lex          kernel/lex/**
    walk         kernel/walk/**
    quire        kernel/quire/**
    // Above `lex` as well as `press`: `impose` reads the scanner to lay a
    // lexer out on the page.
    folio        folio/**
    // The two integration tests, which check the press by running the whole job
    // through the shop rather than by reading the press alone. They sit here,
    // above everything they run, because that is what they are; the four ratified
    // variances this file used to carry were the cost of their old address.
    docket       press/docket/**
    joint        kernel/joint/**
    spine        kernel/spine/**
    weave        kernel/weave/**
    // No zone for the two files at the module root: `root.zig` and `proof.zig`
    // are the package's declared faces, which stands them above every zone here
    // rather than in one of them.
    // A new area's zone goes here, below `abi` — above every zone it reads and
    // below the two faces, which read everything. That is where all three areas
    // with a reserved folio section land: a query engine reads `folio` and
    // `quire`, recovery reads `quire` and `spine`, a quotient reads `press`. None
    // of them is read *by* anything below, because nothing below knows they
    // exist yet, so none of them needs a position among the zones above.
    //
    // This is a comment and not three declared-in-advance zones on purpose: a
    // zone whose glob matches nothing is refused ("the debt was paid; delete
    // them"), and rightly — the header of this file promises a description of the
    // tree as it stands. So the ordering is what gets written down, since the
    // ordering is the part worth not re-deriving. Two facts to save a fourth
    // reading of the manual: zone paths must partition the module, so a new
    // directory needs a zone the day it appears rather than the day it has an
    // import; and `limit reach to 2 hops` means a new area two directories deep
    // reaching `press` is a violation, not a variance to ratify.
    abi          surface/abi/**
    face_joints  surface/face/joints/**
}

// Outside modules, and who carries them.
use build_options by abi face_joints
// The lexer's engine, and — through `irregex.assay` — the instrumentation floor
// this package times, counts, and reports on. Unscoped, because the facade
// itself re-exports `irregex.assay` and declares the package's brand to the
// engine, and a facade has no zone a scoped grant could name: the public entry
// point carrying the dependency IS the package carrying it. (`press` and `lex`
// need the regex engine, `joint` the diagnostic channel, `weave` both.)
use irregex
use joints by abi face_joints

// Structural laws. The reach ceiling is what the tree needs today — lower it
// when a file moves nearer what it depends on; never raise it to go green.
limit  reach to 2 hops
forbid cycles across directories

// The two areas whose interiors are now layered are also the two with a door
// worth defending. Both facades were measured before they were written — 29
// symbols out of 175 reachable for the press, 28 out of 129 for the scanner — and
// a measurement is only a description until something makes it hold. Before these
// two lines, four call sites entered the press around the side; they are through
// `press.zig` now, and the seal is what stops the fifth.
seal press through press.zig
seal kernel/lex through scanner.zig

// No ratified exceptions. There were five, then four, and now none.
//
// The five-way `variance cycle` went first: `folio -> press/press.zig ->
// press/carry_test.zig -> folio` was never a cycle in the program, only in how
// Zig finds tests, since a test is reachable to `zig build test` only if
// something imports it and the only importer on offer was production code.
// `src/proof.zig` is that importer now and `tool/roll.py` is the gate that it
// stays complete, so `press` production files are held to reading nothing above
// them.
//
// The four `variance zone` edges that remained were all one direction out of two
// integration tests wearing a unit test's address, and retiring them was always
// going to mean giving those two files a zone of their own above `folio`. What
// stood in the way was that zone paths must partition the module, so a test
// could not be zoned by what it is while it sat loose in a directory beside what
// it tests. Splitting `press/` into its four real layers removed that: once the
// area has interior directories, `docket/` is just one more of them, and the
// colocation nothing was willing to pay for turns out to cost nothing at all.

// `zoning status` lists the directories that could be sealed next.
